  "summary": "Regional variant of Romanesque architecture characterized by decorative brickwork and distinctive bell towers.",
  "description": "Features extensive use of brick, decorative arcading, and elaborate façades with sculpted elements. Developed from local building traditions and Byzantine influences, creating a distinctive Lombard aesthetic.",
  "culturalContext": "Lombard Romanesque (11th-12th c.) — the Po Valley's brick-and-stone style — spread the \"Lombard band\" (hanging dwarf-gallery arcading), corbel tables and paired pilasters across Italy, Spain's Camino de Santiago and the Rhineland: Modena's cathedral, Sant'Ambrogio Milan, Pavia's San Michele. Wiligelmo's Modena sculpture revived monumental figural carving in Italy; the style's brick craft and gallery skyline defined northern-Italian civic-religious identity for two centuries.",
